        "exit-survey": {
            "kind": "exp-lookit-exit-survey",
            "debriefing": {
                "text": "<strong>Purpose:</strong> You and your baby are helping us to better understand what types of sounds and speakers infants prefer to listen to, and how their preference develops from 6-10 months of age. A previously proposed theory called the Infant Talker Bias says that infants prefer listening to other infants speak. Our study hopes to find out if this bias for younger talkers expands to other social communication, such as laughter. Figuring this out can help us understand exactly what it is about infant speech that is so interesting to babies! \n\n<strong>What your baby did:</strong> In this study, your baby was shown 12 recordings. In six of the recordings, an adult was making sounds and in the other six recordings, an infant was making sounds. Half of the babies in this study were shown speech sounds and the other half were shown laughter sounds. During the videos, we were recording and analyzing how long your baby spent looking at each recording. Looking time is an indicator of the preference of the baby. If your baby spent more time looking at the recordings of infant sounds than they did looking at the recordings of adult sounds, we will be able to suggest that babies of this age have a preference for infant sounds. This suggests that there is something about infant speech that is more interesting to babies than adult speech.  These are the results we are expecting to see from this study. \n\nIt is important to note that there were no right or wrong answers in this study, we just wanted to see what your unique baby preferred! Do not worry if your baby got fussy halfway through and quit looking at the screen, we wanted to record their natural behavior. \n\n<strong>Compensation:</strong> To thank you for your participation, here is a copy of a certificate that you may download for your infant to remember how they were a research assistant before they were even one year old! Just follow the link, from which you can download and print out the certificate <a href='https://utdallas.box.com/s/0y769fprecjfz4yayq12gd0xqmohmpw8' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>here</a>. \n\n<strong>Want to learn more?</strong> Take a look at these TED Talks if you want to learn more about language and laughter development! \n<a href='https://www.ted.com/talks/sophie_scott_why_we_should_take_laughter_more_seriously' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Why we should take laughter more seriously</a> \n<a href='https://www.ted.com/talks/patricia_kuhl_the_linguistic_genius_of_babies' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>The lingustic genius of babies</a>",
                "title": "Thank you for participating in our study!"
            }
        }
